{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe `rebar-janitor` discovery phase (`examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md`)\ncovers tests only as \"smelly/missing tests\" under concern #7 (`:51-52`, operationalised by an `rg`\ncount of test files with fewer than two assertions at `references/patterns.md:116-118`) and as\nchange-detector / duplication / dead-path health under concern #11 (`:119-132`). Neither asks the\ndecisive question: **can this assertion fail when the contract is violated?** There is no concern\nfor oracle strength, and none for contract-coverage gaps — a documented promise no test fails on.\n`phases/verification.md:115` anchors `prod_impact = none` at \"docs/test-only\", so a weak-test\nfinding starts below the impact floor and is dropped even when the contract it fails to guard is\nproduction-critical.\n\nSix weak-oracle bugs escaped in the 7–21 Aug 2026 window — `obvious-saline-gerbil`\n(`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e`), `sound-bumpkinly-thrush` (`1241-b83c-f8c7-40bf`),\n`tapestried-abhorrent-tuna` (`9bb7-4430-1202-4d93`), `lowcost-unsainted-wallaby`\n(`50b1-e181-3716-482c`), `whacky-cadaverous-coati` (`f94e-cf09-fed7-422d`) — none of which a\npre-bug janitor run would have surfaced.\n\nThe rubric that names these classes already exists at `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`\n(the `fail-open oracle` antipattern at `:56-70`, `unrealistic fault injection` at `:45-49`, and the\nten false-positive guards at `:71-106`), but it runs **only on a diff** — never on the resting tree.\nThe gap is therefore reach, not vocabulary: the classes are already articulated, and concern #12\napplies them where diff review cannot. Approved 2026-08-21. Project testing philosophy:\nbehavioural and contractual testing, not coverage percentage.\n\n## Approach\nAdd discovery concern **#12 \"Oracle strength and contract coverage\"** to\n`phases/discovery.md`, sharded by test-tree region as #11 is (`:131-132`), and cross-referenced\nfrom #11.\n\n**Brief to the discovery subagent.** For each test, name the contract under test (docstring, ADR,\n`--help` text, schema statement) and the oracle (its assertion set). Report a finding when the\noracle cannot fail on that contract's violation, proven by naming a concrete `escape_input` — a\nstate the oracle accepts but the contract forbids. Also report contract-coverage gaps: a documented\npromise for which no test fails when it is broken, again proven by naming the violating input.\n\n**Antipattern list — reference, do not restate, with a portable fallback.** When\n`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` is present (a rebar checkout), apply its antipattern\nand false-positive-guard lists by reference; that file is the single source of truth and concern #12\nmust not duplicate it. Because rebar-janitor also runs against repos that do not ship that file,\n`references/patterns.md` carries a **short portable core** — the oracle-strength question, the\n`escape_input` requirement, and the false-positive floor — that concern #12 falls back to. The\nexisting rubric ALREADY covers, and concern #12 must therefore NOT re-declare as new:\n- absent-string / empty-capture assertions with no producer-liveness check → `fail-open oracle`,\n  `code-review-tests.md:56-70`;\n- a race/concurrency test whose primary synchronization is a timing sleep →\n  `code-review-tests.md:49`;\n- every do-not-flag entry below → guards (ii)-(x), `code-review-tests.md:71-106`.\n\n**Genuinely additional, project-specific instances** to name in #12 (verified absent from the\nrubric):\n- a fixture mutating state it did not create;\n- an equivalence test whose two sides use different hardening helpers;\n- an oracle encoding a policy preference the contract does not state.\n\n**Evidence shape.** Extend the discovery output schema with `contract` (quote + `path:line`),\n`oracle` (quote + `path:line`), `escape_input`, `antipattern_id` (an id from\n`code-review-tests.md` where one applies, else the portable-core id), and `scope` (sibling-site\ncount). Update `discovery.md:152` from \"eleven concerns\" to \"twelve\".\n\n**Verification.** Amend `phases/verification.md:115` so a test-only finding takes `prod_impact`\nfrom the **production contract it fails to guard**, not from the fact that the file lives under\n`tests/`. `harm_reachable` for a #12 finding requires a tree-state demonstration — run against a\ndeliberately broken stub, or reason the `escape_input` through the assertion — otherwise the\nexisting reclassification at `verification.md:49-50` applies and it is `HARDENING`, not `DEFECT`.\n\n**Do not flag** (all already guards in `code-review-tests.md:71-106`; #12 cites them, does not\nrestate them): mocks at external boundaries; exact-value asserts on pure functions; exit-code or\nstdout greps of the executed unit whose producer liveness is established; Rule-5 structural greps\non instruction artifacts; strict `xfail` with a named removal condition; secondary settling sleeps\nbeside a deterministic primary mechanism; bare \"no test for X\" with no violating input.\n\n**Feedback loop.** When a #12 finding is a class the diff rubric lacks, the Phase-5 remediation\nticket adds it to `code-review-tests.md` so diff review catches it prospectively.\n\n**SKILL.md frontmatter.** The `description` currently enumerates the discovery concerns and is 980\ncharacters against the 1024 cap enforced by `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py`. Any mention of\noracle strength there must fit in the remaining headroom or displace existing wording.\n\n## Scope\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md` — concern #12, the #11 cross-reference,\n  the schema fields, and the \"eleven\"→\"twelve\" count at `:152`.\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/verification.md` — the `prod_impact` anchor at `:115`.\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/references/patterns.md` — the portable antipattern core and\n  any #12 detection query.\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/SKILL.md` — frontmatter `description` only if it still fits\n  under the 1024-character cap.\n\nNot in scope: no `docs/` page documents the janitor concern list (`grep -rln \"janitor\" docs/` hits\nonly `repo-snapshot-gates.md`, `config.md`, three ADRs, and an experiment corpus).\n`tests/unit/test_check_skill_frontmatter.py` (the actual skill lint test — there is no\n`tests/unit/test_skill_frontmatter.py`) needs no edit; it tests the script, not skill bodies.\n`tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py:16-17` explicitly excludes rebar-janitor, so no shared-copy\nsync is implicated.\n\n## Testing\nSkill documentation change; no production code path changes. `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py`\nvalidates only `**/SKILL.md` frontmatter, so it covers the `description` edit and nothing else —\nthe phase-file and reference-file edits are proven by the greps in the acceptance criteria.\n`pytest tests/unit/test_check_skill_frontmatter.py -q` and `make lint` pass.\n\nDry run (operator-attested, not a gate): execute concern #12 over `tests/unit` on the pre-fix tree\nof `obvious-saline-gerbil` (`git checkout 6fb4d801c9^`) and confirm the fail-open absent-string\noracle is reported with a concrete `escape_input`. Recorded as a transcript comment on this ticket\nand marked `[operator-attested]`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `discovery.md` carries concern #12 with its title: `grep -qE '^12\\. \\*\\*Oracle strength and contract coverage\\*\\*' examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md` exits 0 (no `12.` line exists today).\n- [ ] Concern #12 requires a named escape input and is sharded like #11: `awk '/^12\\. \\*\\*Oracle strength/,/^### Temporal decay pass/' examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md | grep -qi \"escape_input\"` and the same range `| grep -qi \"shard\"` both exit 0.\n- [ ] Concern #12 references the diff rubric rather than duplicating it, and names a portable fallback: `awk '/^12\\. \\*\\*Oracle strength/,/^### Temporal decay pass/' examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md | grep -q \"code-review-tests.md\"` exits 0, and the same range contains no re-declaration of the covered classes — `| grep -ciE \"fail-open oracle|strict xfail|settling\"` returns `0` (they are cited by reference only).\n- [ ] The portable fallback exists in the skill's own tree: `grep -qi \"oracle\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/references/patterns.md` exits 0.\n- [ ] The output schema gained the #12 fields: `for f in contract oracle escape_input antipattern_id scope; do grep -q \"^- \\`$f\\`\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md || { echo \"MISSING $f\"; exit 1; }; done` exits 0.\n- [ ] The concern count is consistent: `grep -c \"which of the eleven concerns above\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md` returns `0` and `grep -q \"which of the twelve concerns above\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md` exits 0.\n- [ ] Concern #11 cross-references #12: `awk '/^11\\. \\*\\*Test-suite health/,/^### Temporal decay pass/' examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md | grep -q \"#12\"` exits 0.\n- [ ] `verification.md` no longer anchors `prod_impact` at \"docs/test-only\" unconditionally: `grep -n \"none = docs/test-only\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/verification.md` returns a line that also names the guarded production contract, or returns nothing and `grep -qi \"guarded production contract\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/verification.md` exits 0.\n- [ ] `verification.md` requires a tree-state demonstration before a #12 finding can be `DEFECT`: `grep -qi \"tree-state demonstration\" examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/verification.md` exits 0.\n- [ ] Frontmatter still lints: `python scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py` exits 0 and reports `PASS: 4 SKILL.md file(s)`.\n- [ ] No collateral edits: `git diff --name-only origin/main` lists only files named in Scope.\n- [ ] Gates pass: `pytest tests/unit/test_check_skill_frontmatter.py tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py -q` and `make lint` exit 0.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] Dry run on `git checkout 6fb4d801c9^` (the `obvious-saline-gerbil` pre-fix tree) reports the fail-open absent-string oracle with a concrete `escape_input`; transcript posted as a comment on this ticket. Non-gating — stochastic subagent output, recorded for calibration, not as a pass/fail criterion.\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-21)\n- Removed the flagship \"new project-specific instance\": an absent-string subprocess assertion with no `returncode`/`check=True` already ships as the `fail-open oracle` antipattern at `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md:56-70`, landed by commit `6fb4d801c9` — the fix for `obvious-saline-gerbil` (`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e`), the very bug the dry run uses. Concern #12 now cites it rather than re-declaring it.\n- Removed the \"wall-clock budget standing in for an observable event\" as new: `code-review-tests.md:49` already flags a concurrency/race test whose PRIMARY synchronization is a timing sleep.\n- Reduced the do-not-flag list to citations: all seven entries were already false-positive guards (ii)-(x) at `code-review-tests.md:71-106` — Rule-5 structural greps (ii, `:82-88`), command-output greps with established liveness (iii, `:91-94`), exact-value asserts on pure functions (iv, `:94-96`), external-boundary mocks (vi, `:96`), bare \"no test for X\" (vii, `:97`), strict `xfail` with a named removal condition (viii, `:99-101`), and secondary settling sleeps (ix, `:102-103`). Restating them contradicted the plan's own \"single source of truth, not restated\" principle.\n- Kept only the three instances verified absent from the rubric: a fixture mutating state it did not create; an equivalence test whose two sides use different hardening helpers; an oracle encoding a policy preference the contract does not state.\n- Corrected the Scope path: `tests/unit/test_skill_frontmatter.py` does not exist. The real file is `tests/unit/test_check_skill_frontmatter.py`, and it needs no edit — it tests the script, not skill bodies.\n- Corrected the Testing claim: `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py` globs `**/SKILL.md` and validates only YAML frontmatter, so it never reads `phases/discovery.md`, `phases/verification.md`, or `references/patterns.md` — three of the four files in `file_impact`. Those edits are now proven by explicit greps in the acceptance criteria instead.\n- Added the missing consumer: `discovery.md:152` hardcodes \"which of the eleven concerns above\" in the output schema handed to every discovery subagent; adding #12 without updating it leaves the schema contradicting the concern list.\n- Added the missing scope item and its constraint: `SKILL.md` is in `file_impact` but had no criterion. Its frontmatter `description` enumerates the concerns and measures 980 characters against the 1024-character cap, leaving 44 characters of headroom.\n- Replaced ACs #1-#3, which were \"checked by reading the file\" and \"checked by the run transcript\" — no command, no oracle. Every criterion is now a command that fails on the current tree; the stochastic dry run is retained as an explicitly non-gating `[operator-attested]` item, carrying the provenance marker `project.measurement-provenance` triggers on.\n- Added the portable antipattern core in `references/patterns.md` (`project.portability` is blocking and active per `.rebar/criteria_routing.json`): rebar-janitor runs against non-rebar repos where `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` does not exist, which would have left concern #12 with no antipattern list at all.\n- Dropped the hedged `docs/` page from Scope: `grep -rln \"janitor\" docs/` confirms no page documents the janitor concern list."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787368869143470001,"uuid":"9651956b-24a9-437d-b3ca-3b8aeb54069e"}